[
  "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The scene features two ceramic plates arranged in a warm, lived-in kitchen environment. The primary plate is centered in the frame, angled at a 35-degree 3/4 overhead perspective to capture the depth of the components, with a second plate softly blurred in the background. Lighting is soft, natural window light from the left, creating subtle shadows and highlights on the soy-glazed vegetables. The surface is a rustic, aged oak wooden table with slight visible wear. The dish consists of a base of fluffy, nutty-brown steamed rice, topped with a vibrant mixture of bright green, crisp-tender sugar snap peas and edamame, and garnished with finely sliced scallions. Two slices of sesame-crusted, medium-rare tuna are arranged neatly alongside the vegetables rather than on top, showcasing the toasted, golden-brown sesame crust and a tender, pink, opaque center. A small, rustic wooden spoon rests beside the foreground plate. A few stray sesame seeds and a small splash of dark soy-based sauce are visible on the ceramic rim. Props include a small ramekin containing a hint of ginger-infused soy sauce placed near the rear plate. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene should look like someone just finished cooking and plating — lived-in, warm, authentic. Not a sterile studio setup. Slight natural imperfections: an asymmetric garnish placement, a small sauce drip on the plate rim, slightly uneven browning. Include subtle traces of the cooking process: a few scattered sesame seeds on the wood, a faint oil mark near the plate. No plastic sheen, no unnaturally smooth surfaces, no perfect symmetry. Warm, golden-hour color temperature — cozy and inviting. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. The image should be indistinguishable from a photograph taken by a professional food photographer for a printed cookbook."
]
